1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,677
Some jobs pay close to a hundred and fifty
thousand dollars a year,

2
00:00:04,717 --> 00:00:07,224
and they still can't find enough people.

3
00:00:07,264 --> 00:00:08,347
Why?

4
00:00:08,387 --> 00:00:10,450
Because of what you have to do to earn it.

5
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:14,599
Here are ten jobs that pay surprisingly
well,

6
00:00:14,639 --> 00:00:17,547
but most people would never take.

7
00:00:17,587 --> 00:00:21,343
All numbers are American median pay, from
the U.S.

8
00:00:21,383 --> 00:00:23,805
Bureau of Labor Statistics.

9
00:00:23,845 --> 00:00:27,970
And number one pays almost three times the
typical job.

10
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:29,554
Number ten.

11
00:00:29,594 --> 00:00:31,836
The wastewater plant operator.

12
00:00:31,876 --> 00:00:35,093
Everything you flush has to go somewhere.

13
00:00:35,133 --> 00:00:41,090
These operators keep the plant running, so
the dirty water comes out clean.

14
00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:46,183
The median pay is about sixty thousand
dollars a year.

15
00:00:46,223 --> 00:00:50,376
The catch: the smell, the noise, dangerous
gases,

16
00:00:50,416 --> 00:00:52,999
and a plant that never stops.

17
00:00:53,039 --> 00:00:55,590
Nights, weekends and holidays included.

18
00:00:56,240 --> 00:00:57,681
Number nine.

19
00:00:57,721 --> 00:00:58,402
The ironworker.

20
00:00:58,442 --> 00:01:02,903
These are the people who walk on narrow
steel beams,

21
00:01:02,943 --> 00:01:07,330
sometimes fifty stories up, bolting
skyscrapers together.

22
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:12,653
Median pay: around sixty-three thousand
dollars.

23
00:01:12,693 --> 00:01:17,560
They wear harnesses, but a fall from that
height can still be deadly.

24
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:21,700
And when it's icy or very windy, the work
has to stop.

25
00:01:22,350 --> 00:01:23,296
Number eight.

26
00:01:23,336 --> 00:01:25,675
The oil rig drill operator.

27
00:01:25,715 --> 00:01:31,147
They run the drill that bores deep into
the ground for oil and gas,

28
00:01:31,187 --> 00:01:33,540
on land or far out at sea.

29
00:01:34,190 --> 00:01:37,914
Median pay: about sixty-eight thousand.

30
00:01:37,954 --> 00:01:44,050
The downside: offshore and remote crews
can be away for weeks at a time,

31
00:01:44,090 --> 00:01:49,210
working long shifts seven days a week, in
the noise and in any weather.

32
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:51,128
Number seven.

33
00:01:51,168 --> 00:01:52,097
The roof bolter.

34
00:01:52,137 --> 00:01:56,796
In an underground mine, before anyone else
can work,

35
00:01:56,836 --> 00:02:00,492
somebody has to stop the ceiling from
falling.

36
00:02:00,532 --> 00:02:04,540
They drill long steel bolts into the rock
above their heads.

37
00:02:05,190 --> 00:02:08,881
They earn around seventy-five thousand
dollars a year.

38
00:02:08,921 --> 00:02:12,279
In the dark, in the dust, far below the
surface,

39
00:02:12,319 --> 00:02:14,760
with the whole mountain sitting on top of
them.

40
00:02:15,410 --> 00:02:16,622
Number six.

41
00:02:16,662 --> 00:02:18,878
The locomotive engineer.

42
00:02:18,918 --> 00:02:22,065
They drive freight trains, some of them
more than a mile long,

43
00:02:22,105 --> 00:02:24,490
across the country.

44
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:28,308
Median pay: about eighty-one thousand.

45
00:02:28,348 --> 00:02:30,423
But trains run day and night.

46
00:02:30,463 --> 00:02:36,313
Schedules can be irregular, long routes
keep you away from home,

47
00:02:36,353 --> 00:02:41,010
and the best shifts go to whoever has the
most seniority.

48
00:02:41,660 --> 00:02:43,184
Number five.

49
00:02:43,224 --> 00:02:44,551
The ship captain.

50
00:02:44,591 --> 00:02:50,082
Captains and mates steer barges, tankers
and cargo ships across lakes,

51
00:02:50,122 --> 00:02:51,660
rivers and oceans.

52
00:02:52,310 --> 00:02:56,114
Median pay: about ninety-two thousand
dollars.

53
00:02:56,154 --> 00:02:59,145
The catch: weeks or even months away from
home,

54
00:02:59,185 --> 00:03:06,350
working seven days a week on board, in any
weather the sea decides to send.

55
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:07,829
Number four.

56
00:03:07,869 --> 00:03:10,830
The power line worker.

57
00:03:10,870 --> 00:03:15,548
When a storm knocks out the power,
everyone else stays inside.

58
00:03:15,588 --> 00:03:17,350
These workers go out.

59
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,614
Median pay: about ninety-five thousand.

60
00:03:21,654 --> 00:03:25,963
They climb poles and towers next to high-
voltage lines,

61
00:03:26,003 --> 00:03:30,850
and after a big storm they can work long
hours, many days in a row.

62
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:32,810
Number three.

63
00:03:32,850 --> 00:03:34,712
The elevator mechanic.

64
00:03:34,752 --> 00:03:39,236
Somebody has to install and fix every
elevator you ride.

65
00:03:39,276 --> 00:03:44,590
They work inside the shafts, on top of the
cars, and in cramped machine rooms.

66
00:03:45,240 --> 00:03:49,850
Median pay: almost one hundred ten
thousand dollars a year.

67
00:03:49,890 --> 00:03:55,652
Many are on call around the clock, because
when an elevator gets stuck,

68
00:03:55,692 --> 00:03:57,850
someone has to come right now.

69
00:03:58,500 --> 00:03:59,977
Number two.

70
00:04:00,017 --> 00:04:02,493
The nuclear reactor operator.

71
00:04:02,533 --> 00:04:07,763
They sit in the control room of a nuclear
power plant and control the reactor

72
00:04:07,803 --> 00:04:08,360
itself.

73
00:04:09,010 --> 00:04:13,424
Median pay: about one hundred twenty-three
thousand.

74
00:04:13,464 --> 00:04:18,778
The downside: rotating day and night
shifts that wreck your sleep,

75
00:04:18,818 --> 00:04:25,800
heavy security, and a job where you must
stay fully alert every single minute.

76
00:04:26,450 --> 00:04:28,062
Number one.

77
00:04:28,102 --> 00:04:29,254
The air traffic controller.

78
00:04:29,294 --> 00:04:34,267
They guide thousands of planes through the
sky every day,

79
00:04:34,307 --> 00:04:36,630
and make sure none of them get too close.

80
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:41,993
Median pay: about one hundred forty-eight
thousand dollars.

81
00:04:42,033 --> 00:04:46,102
That's almost three times the typical
American job.

82
00:04:46,142 --> 00:04:49,478
But the concentration never stops,

83
00:04:49,518 --> 00:04:54,360
and the stress is so high that controllers
must retire at fifty-six.

84
00:04:55,010 --> 00:04:57,828
And right now there aren't enough of them.

85
00:04:57,868 --> 00:05:03,018
Reports put America about three thousand
air traffic controllers short,

86
00:05:03,058 --> 00:05:06,640
and many work ten-hour days, six days a
week.

87
00:05:07,290 --> 00:05:10,521
So why don't more people take these jobs?

88
00:05:10,561 --> 00:05:11,687
Look at the pattern.

89
00:05:11,727 --> 00:05:13,454
Heights.

90
00:05:13,494 --> 00:05:14,261
Darkness.

91
00:05:14,301 --> 00:05:14,421
Danger.

92
00:05:14,461 --> 00:05:15,990
Bad smells.

93
00:05:16,030 --> 00:05:16,445
Night shifts.

94
00:05:16,485 --> 00:05:17,498
Weeks away from home.

95
00:05:17,538 --> 00:05:21,800
The pay is high because the price is high.

96
00:05:22,450 --> 00:05:26,473
And most of these jobs don't need a
university degree.

97
00:05:26,513 --> 00:05:28,085
They need training, steady nerves,

98
00:05:28,125 --> 00:05:31,220
and a willingness to do what other people
won't.

99
00:05:31,870 --> 00:05:36,494
So if you're choosing a career, ask two
questions, not one.

100
00:05:36,534 --> 00:05:38,035
How much does it pay?

101
00:05:38,075 --> 00:05:39,356
And what does it cost you?

102
00:05:39,396 --> 00:05:43,060
For some people, the trade is worth it.

103
00:05:43,710 --> 00:05:46,927
Which of these would you do for the money?

104
00:05:46,967 --> 00:05:48,445
Tell us in the comments.

105
00:05:48,486 --> 00:05:51,441
And if you liked this, subscribe.

106
00:05:51,481 --> 00:05:55,230
Next time: the jobs that might disappear
in the next ten years.

